---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="OZ 1.0 Retrospective" description="What the evidence says about OZ 1.0 outcomes — investment patterns, community impact, and lessons for OZ 2.0.">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">OZ 1.0 Retrospective</h1>
    <p class="text-oz-neutral-500 mb-8">
      The original Opportunity Zone program ran from 2018 under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.
      A body of academic research, government assessments, and community development critiques
      now exists on what it did and didn't accomplish. OZ 2.0 was designed in explicit response
      to those findings. This page summarizes the evidence honestly.
    </p>

    <section id="investment-data" class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">What the investment data showed<a href="#investment-data" class="anchor-link" aria-label="Copy link to section"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg></a></h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700 mb-4">
        Significant private capital did flow into OZ-designated tracts. Qualified Opportunity Funds
        (QOFs) self-certified with the IRS via Form 8996, and Treasury's filings showed meaningful
        fund formation and asset accumulation. The Trump White House Council of Economic Advisers,
        in its
        <a href="https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/The-Impact-of-Opportunity-Zones-An-Initial-Assessment.pdf" class="text-oz-blue underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">August 2020 initial assessment</a>,
        characterized the program as generating substantial investment and job creation in
        designated areas. Treasury economists Coyne and Johnson, with access to the full Form 8996
        universe, documented approximately $48 billion in total QOF assets by end of 2020 — rising
        from $4 billion in 2018 to $28 billion in 2019. Their
        <a href="https://www.novoco.com/public-media/documents/office-tax-analysis-working-paper-06-2026-wp-128.pdf" class="text-oz-blue underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">June 2026 standalone update (OTA Working Paper 128)</a>
        extends that series through tax year 2024: cumulative qualified investment reached
        $112 billion, with total QOF assets of $116 billion across roughly 12,800 funds — the
        most current picture of OZ 1.0 capital that exists, and the last before OZ 2.0
        designations take effect.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700 mb-4">
        The geographic distribution of that capital, however, was skewed. Kennedy and Wheeler,
        using Form 8996 data accessed via the Joint Committee on Taxation, found that only 1,347
        of 8,764 designated OZ tracts received any investment in 2019 — and the top 1% of tracts
        absorbed 42% of all capital, with the top 5% taking 78%. By 2020 coverage had broadened
        (3,242 tracts funded), but 63% of OZ tracts still received zero investment from electronic
        filers. Coyne and Johnson found that 84% of all QOZ investment was concentrated in the
        top 20% of communities that received any investment at all — and their 2026 update shows
        the skew persisted at scale: through tax year 2024, the top 20% of funded tracts held
        about 90% of all qualifying investment. Investment concentrated in
        already-appreciating urban tracts, not the high-distress rural and legacy industrial
        communities the program was conceived to help.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700 mb-4">
        The absence of enforceable disclosure requirements under OZ 1.0 made it difficult to
        assess outcomes precisely. QOFs filed Form 8996 but faced no meaningful penalty for
        non-filing or incomplete data. Coyne and Johnson's analysis — the most comprehensive
        ever done using the full IRS data — estimated roughly 21,000 individual investors in
        2020, with a median adjusted gross income of approximately $741,000, compared to a
        national median household income of $69,000 (Kennedy &amp; Wheeler, using IRS records
        for the same period). The investor profile barely moved over the program's life: by tax
        year 2024, roughly 41,000 taxpayers reported deferred gains held in QOFs, and the
        typical individual filer had an AGI of $738,000 — against $54,000 for the typical
        Form 1040 filer (WP-128). Individuals, not corporations, remained the dominant
        investors, accounting for about 85% of Forms 8997 and over 60% of deferred gains in
        both 2020 and 2024 — a sharp contrast with the NMTC, where nearly all qualifying
        investment comes from corporations.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700 mb-4">
        The JCT staff's May 2024 analysis, drawing on electronically filed returns through tax
        year 2022, documented $84.7 billion in cumulative QOF investment — of which $77.2 billion
        could be matched to specific census tracts. Only 8.5 percent of that tract-matched
        investment reached rural areas, and 94 percent of tracts in the top investment decile
        were metropolitan. These figures confirm that OZ 1.0 capital formation continued steadily
        through the program's TCJA-era lifespan while the metro concentration problem
        documented in 2019–2020 persisted.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-oz-neutral-700">
        Treasury's 2026 update closes out the record with both broader reach and persistent
        concentration. By end of tax year 2024, 77 percent of designated OZs in the 50 states
        and DC had received some qualified investment — up from 48 percent through 2020. But
        23 percent of tracts (just over 1,800) still received nothing, another 34 percent
        received less than $1 million, and the top 20 percent of funded tracts held about
        90 percent of the total. Rural tracts — 38 percent of all OZs under the OBBBA rural
        definition — were funded at the same 77 percent rate as OZs overall and grew their
        share of investment from 12.6 to 16.1 percent, but the average funded rural OZ
        received $7.3 million against $23.3 million for the average funded non-rural OZ.
        Ranked on the Gelfond–Looney economic distress index, the most distressed deciles of
        OZs received well below an equal share of investment, while the least-distressed
        decile received more than double its share.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="by-the-numbers" class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">By the numbers<a href="#by-the-numbers" class="anchor-link" aria-label="Copy link to section"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg></a></h2>

      <!-- Source context -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 mb-6 text-xs">
        <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-3 bg-white">
          <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">Kennedy &amp; Wheeler (2022)</p>
          <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">Form 8996 e-filers via JCT · Tax years 2019–2020 · ~78% of total $ volume. Commuting zone geography. Best source for sub-state concentration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-3 bg-white">
          <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">Coyne &amp; Johnson (Treasury OTA, 2023)</p>
          <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">Full Form 8996 universe + Form 8997 investor data · Tax years 2018–2020. Most comprehensive investor-level analysis. State geography.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-3 bg-white">
          <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">JCT Staff (May 2024)</p>
          <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">E-filers · Tax years 2019–2022 · All 50 states, DC + territories. State investment, rural splits, sector breakdown (NAICS), investor income distribution.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-3 bg-white">
          <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">Coyne &amp; Johnson (Treasury OTA WP-128, June 2026)</p>
          <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">Full Form 8996 universe + Form 8997 · Tax years 2018–2024 (returns as of Apr 30, 2026). Standalone update of the 2023 paper; final pre-OZ-2.0 record. Uses the OBBBA rural definition.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Investment timeline -->
      <div class="mb-7">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-oz-neutral-500 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">Cumulative QOZ property held by QOFs, 2018–2024 — Coyne &amp; Johnson (Treasury OTA WP-128, June 2026)</p>
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2018</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 4%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0 text-xs">$4B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2019</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 25%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 shrink-0">$28B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2020</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 39%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 shrink-0">$44B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2021</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 59%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 shrink-0">$66B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2022</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 81%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 shrink-0">$91B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2023</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 94%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 shrink-0">$105B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
            <span class="w-10 text-oz-neutral-500 shrink-0">2024</span>
            <div class="flex-1 bg-oz-neutral-100 rounded-full h-5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="h-full rounded-full bg-[#1a4480]" style="width: 100%;"></div>
            </div>
            <span class="w-16 text-right font-semibold text-oz-neutral-900 shrink-0">$112B</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-400 mt-2">Full Form 8996 universe (paper + electronic), rounded totals as of April 30, 2026 filings. The 2021 spike ($44B → $66B, +50% in one year) reflects the December 31, 2021 effective deadline to qualify for the 5-year 10% basis step-up — yet investment kept growing after that benefit lapsed, driven by new deferrals, debt and other non-gain financing, and asset revaluation. JCT's May 2024 figure for 2022 ($84.7B) is lower than WP-128's $91B mainly because of returns processed after JCT's cutoff. Deferred gains on Form 8997 ($75B in 2024) fell from ~81% of QOF total assets in 2020 to ~65% in 2024 — a sign of the growing role of debt financing alongside deferred gains.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Concentration stats -->
      <div class="mb-7">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-oz-neutral-500 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">Geographic concentration</p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3">
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-4 text-center bg-white">
            <div class="text-4xl font-bold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">42%</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-500 leading-snug">of all capital went to<br>the top 1% of OZ tracts</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-400 mt-1">K&amp;W · 2020 e-filers</div>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-4 text-center bg-white">
            <div class="text-4xl font-bold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">78%</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-500 leading-snug">of all capital went to<br>the top 5% of OZ tracts</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-400 mt-1">K&amp;W · 2020 e-filers</div>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-200 rounded p-4 text-center bg-white">
            <div class="text-4xl font-bold text-oz-neutral-900 mb-1">~90%</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-500 leading-snug">of QOZ investment in<br>the top 20% of funded OZs</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-oz-neutral-400 mt-1">C&amp;J · full universe through 2024</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs">
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-100 rounded p-3 bg-oz-neutral-50">
            <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-700 mb-1">Tract coverage improved over time</p>
            <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">In 2019: 1,347 of 8,764 OZ tracts received any investment (15% of e-filers). By end of 2020: 48% (C&amp;J full universe). By end of 2024: 77% — but 23% of tracts (just over 1,800) still showed zero reported investment, and another 34% received less than $1M total (WP-128).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-100 rounded p-3 bg-oz-neutral-50">
            <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-700 mb-1">Within-state income skew</p>
            <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">Through 2024, 35% of QOZ investment went to the highest-income quintile of designated OZ tracts within each state — roughly double the 17% share that went to the lowest-income quintile (WP-128 Table 17). State patterns diverged sharply: DC put 72% of its investment in its poorest quintile; Utah, Idaho, Montana, Connecticut, and Hawaii put over 65% in their richest.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs">
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-100 rounded p-3 bg-oz-neutral-50">
            <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-700 mb-1">Rural share of investment: 16.1% by 2024</p>
            <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">Under the OBBBA rural definition (tract doesn't overlap an urban area), rural tracts are 37.9% of OZs but received 16.1% of QOZ property through 2024 — up from 12.6% in 2020. Rural OZs were funded at the same 77% rate as OZs overall; they simply received far less per tract: $7.3M per funded rural OZ vs. $23.3M non-rural (WP-128 Tables 10, 12). JCT's narrower non-metro measure put the rural share at 8.5% through 2022. The OZ 2.0 rural carve-out (QROF) is a direct response to this gap.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-oz-neutral-100 rounded p-3 bg-oz-neutral-50">
            <p class="font-semibold text-oz-neutral-700 mb-1">Metro dominance of top-funded tracts: 94%</p>
            <p class="text-oz-neutral-500">94% of tracts in the top investment decile (by 2022 cumulative investment) are metropolitan — compared to 76.6% of all designated OZ tracts and 83.5% of all U.S. census tracts. The metro/rural gap in investment intensity was largest at the top of the distribution. (JCT Table 3, 2024)</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

